export PATH=$PATH:/c/PKS-CLI:/c/kubectl:/c/helm
Docker Image: kubectl get deployments -n ent-prod -o custom-columns="DEPLOYMENT:.metadata.name,IMAGE:.spec.template.spec.containers[*].image"
KIRO Login: https://charter-cloud-connect.awsapps.com/start (USE login with amazon and use east zone)
**alias kubectl='winpty kubectl'  --> to connect from gitbash to a pod

Network/Firewall connectivity:
**Telnet in pod: apk add busybox-extras
**Curl: apk add curl (if not installing, first install telnet then curl)
** kubectl get endpoints -n ocbo-sit  ---> to get the endpoints for connectivity test


PODS/Deployments:
**kubectl get ns ---> To get namespaces
**kubectl get deployment -n ocbofms-dev    ----> To get deployments details
**kubectl get po camunda-756f787c6b-fjkhv -c istio-proxy -n devops  --> Describe container pod
**kubectl top pods -A                          --> To check pod CPU, MEMORY
**kubectl get pods --all-namespaces --> List of pods on all namespaces                                                 
**kubectl get pods -A                           --> List of pods 
**kubectl get pods -A | grep -i init     -->(<Name: init container>)
**kubectl get po -n <namespace>     --> List of pods o particular namespace 
**kubectl describe pod <Name> -n <Name Space> ---> It will describe pod complete status
**helm ls -n ocbo-nce-prod                 --> To get list of previous packages
**helm history camunda -n ocbo-dev  --> Helm history
**kubectl logs <podname> -c istio-proxy -n <namespace>  --> Pod Logs
**kubectl logs -f <podname> -n <namespace> ---> To fetch the logs of a particular pod
**kubectl logs -f camunda-68c76fd465-x75rv -n camunda-dev | grep -i error  --> Using GREP it will show only error logs
**kubectl get pod -n <namespace> -o wide ---> To fetch all the pods in a namespace(we can check IP address)
**kubectl exec -it camunda-756f787c6b-fjkhv -c istio-proxy -n devops -- sh --> container/pod logging
**kubectl exec -it ocbo-6f469c4fdc-fzrqs -n ocbo-dev  /bin/sh or -- bash or -- sh or bash   ----> To go inside the container/pod
**kubectl get pods -n ocbo-nce-prod -o wide  ---> To see which node is assigned on particular pod
**kubectl get po fms-logging-758dd85d4f-8m9kv -n ocbofms-dev -o yaml ---> To open yaml  ctrl+c  to exit file
** kubectl get pod ocbo-heliosproxy-54f9587fd9-c2442 -n ocbo-heliosproxy-dev -o yaml ---> To view on yaml file, particular docker image is running etc
**kubectl cluster-info ---> to get cluster info


Nodes:
** kubectl get nodes          --> List of nodes
** kubectl top nodes dev  ---> To get the load, CPU utilization, memory


Ingress/ISTIO:
**kubectl get ingress -A ---> To check IP address and port nos
**kubectl get ingress -n ocbofms-dev ---> To check IP address of namespaces
** kubectl get ingress fms-logging -n ocbofms-dev -o yaml   ---> To view ingress yaml file
**kubectl describe ingress fms-logging -n ocbofms-dev  --> Describe Ingress
**kubectl get ing -A --> To get the dns name
**kubectl describe ingress fms-logging -n ocbofms-dev ----> To get DNS name 

Secrets:
**kubectl get secrets -n ocbo-nemesis-svc-dev --> Retrieve Secrets
**kubectl get secrets ocbo-nemesis-svc-token-mfw7p -n ocbo-nemesis-svc-dev -o yaml --> Describe Secrets

Replicas:
**kubectl scale --replicas=0 deployment camunda-test -n optimize-test  ---> To scale down the particular deployment
**kubectl scale --current-replicas=9 --replicas=12 deployment/ceis-camunda -n ocbo-nce-prod
  ex: camunda-test, elastic search are two deployments in one namespace, If you want to scale down camunda the keep replicas=0 and if you want to see again keep replicas=1  
